{"id": "tea:anandaghana-covisi:1", "source": "src:anandaghana-covisi", "location": {"ref": "1", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Ṛṣabha, lord Jina, is my beloved; I want no other husband; once pleased he never leaves me — a love with no beginning and no end.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "devotional", "naya": null, "path": ["devotion"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice", "ultimate"], "concepts": ["cpt:adhyatma-bhakti"], "teachers": ["tch:anandaghana", "tch:rsabha"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Opening of the first stavana, recalled in substance.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:anandaghana-covisi:2", "source": "src:anandaghana-covisi", "location": {"ref": "2", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The path of the Jina is hard to see; with human eyes it cannot be found, and argument, tradition and scriptures quarrel over it; only the divine eye of experience reveals it.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge", "devotion"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["teacher-transmission", "dispute"], "concepts": ["cpt:adhyatma-bhakti"], "teachers": ["tch:anandaghana"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Second stavana (to Ajitanātha); recalled in substance.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:anandaghana-covisi:21", "source": "src:anandaghana-covisi", "location": {"ref": "21", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The six darśanas are called the limbs of the Jina: Sāṃkhya and Yoga his two feet, the Buddhist and the Mīmāṃsaka his two hands, the Lokāyata [the belly], and the Jaina his head; whoever unites the six limbs is the true seeker.", "tags": {"level": "bridging", "standpoint": "devotional", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge", "devotion"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ultimate", "dispute"], "concepts": ["cpt:jain-views-of-other-darsanas", "cpt:anekantavada"], "teachers": ["tch:anandaghana"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "21st stavana (to Naminātha); the assignment of limbs is recalled and may be inexact.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
